#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096446 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc49f831-8e1d-5fb4-8952-1dd4ee3bfa02" :knowledge-point/title "Creating Parallel Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "d1329498-4413-5d87-a814-be0d57a3afd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4280" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf a$ is shown in the diagram below. Draw the vectors $2\\mathbf a$ and $-3\\mathbf a$.\n\n![](images/a5/a5eaa8baaf303ecbfa6c0fb207d6b19836cc90fc331e238cd473109f44fddf80.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2\\mathbf{a}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\n![](images/41/41eba7e6c5bbe420418726bd45efa668dc6be5ff5144ba35d52b53a43925c333.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-3\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3\\mathbf{a}$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-3$ is negative.\n\n![](images/ed/eda3f2572d98ef1789292ec6d35569487b32efa459673dcb95f8fdd0d1f4ae5b.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc49f831-8e1d-5fb4-8952-1dd4ee3bfa02" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049603}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504023 :question-generator/id #uuid "3b8d388a-714a-55a4-8f2e-330d279175ef" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186096649.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc49f831-8e1d-5fb4-8952-1dd4ee3bfa02" :knowledge-point/title "Creating Parallel Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84bcbe16-7536-5125-b103-ef9c458c